<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chittor Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chittorgarh, Chittorgarh Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chittor Fort, or Chittorgarh, is a hill fort above the Berach River in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and one of the largest forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the chief seat of the Guhila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> rulers of Mewar until 1568 and fell three times, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> in 1303, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a> in 1535 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in 1568, each fall remembered for the jauhar of its women. Its monuments include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijay-stambha">Vijay Stambha</a>, and it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chittor-fort.jpg" alt="Chittor Fort" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssjoshi111 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chittorgarh_fort.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The fort stands on a hill that rises abruptly 180 m above the plains on the right bank of the Berach River, a tributary of the Banas. Its walls run for 13 km round a fish-shaped plateau about 5 km long. Seven gates built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>, who ruled Mewar from 1433 to 1468, lead up the slope from the Paidal Pol at the base to the Ram Pol, the main gate. The fort once held 84 water bodies, of which 22 remain, and their storage was said to meet the needs of an army of 50,000 for four years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition holds that the fort was first called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitrakoot">Chitrakut</a> and was built by a Mori ruler, Chitrangada Mori. The Guhila ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bappa-rawal">Bappa Rawal</a> is said to have taken it in 728 or 734, though some historians doubt that the Guhilas held Chittor before the reign of Allata. In 1303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> took the fort after a siege of eight months, and his courtier Amir Khusrow wrote that he ordered a massacre of 30,000 local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. Most modern historians reject the later legends that he came for the queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmini">Padmini</a>. Rajput rule was restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammir-singh">Hammir Singh</a>, from whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> line took its name, and the fort was at its most powerful under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a>, who ruled from 1509 until January 1528.</p><p>Bahadur Shah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, besieged the fort in 1535, and tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rani-karnavati">Rani Karnavati</a> led 13,000 women in the jauhar while 3,200 warriors rode out to die. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> camped before the fort on 20 October 1567, after Rana Udai Singh II had withdrawn to Gogunda, leaving Jaimal and Patta with 8,000 soldiers. The siege lasted four months. Jaimal was killed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> shot on 22 February 1568, and the next day the defenders rushed out. They died with 20,000 to 25,000 civilians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> returned the fort to Amar Singh in 1615.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fort holds 65 historic structures, among them four palace complexes, 19 main temples and four memorials. The Vijaya Stambha, a nine-storey tower of 37.2 m, was raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> between 1458 and 1468 for his victory over Mahmud Shah I Khalji of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1440. The older Kirti Stambha, probably of the 12th century, is dedicated to the Jain teacher Adinath. In 2013 the fort was inscribed with five other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chittor Fort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dynastic tradition makes Bappa Rawal, who is said to have taken the great fort of <b>Chittor</b> from its Mori rulers in the early eighth century, the true founder, and the register’s date of 728 follows this tradition; whether Bappa is the same person as a ruler named in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alauddin Khalji of the Delhi Sultanate entered <b>Chittor Fort</b>, taken from the Guhila king Ratnasimha, and ordered a general massacre of its people; Amir Khusrau put the dead at 30,000, a figure one historian calls probably an exaggeration.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
